  "worked_solution": "We must find the maximum value of $f(x,y)$ subject to the constraint $g(x,y)=2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y=0.$ \n\nSince $f(x,y)$ is continuous and the constraint is closed and bounded, $f(x,y)$ reaches a global maximum and a global minimum on the constraint. \n\nTherefore, by the method of Lagrange multipliers, we must solve the following system:\n\n$$\n∇f(x,y)=\\lambda ∇g(x,y),\\,g(x,y)=0\n$$\n\nWe start by solving the first equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & =\\lambda ∇g(x,y) \\\\ ⟨{f}_{x}(x,y),{f}_{y}(x,y)⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨{g}_{x}(x,y),{g}_{y}(x,y)⟩ \\\\ ⟨-2x,-6y⟩ & =\\lambda ⟨4x,2y-2⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}-2x=4\\lambda x \\\\ -6y=2\\lambda (y-1)\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}x(1+2\\lambda )=0 \\\\ y(\\lambda +3)=\\lambda \\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTo solve this system, we break it up into several cases:\n\n- Assuming $x=0$ in the first equation of the system, the constraint condition $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-2y & =0\\,⇒\\,y=0,2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives the critical points $(0,0),(0,2).$\n\n- Assuming $\\lambda =-\\frac{1}{2}$ in the first equation of the system, the second equation of the system implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y(-\\frac{1}{2}+3)=-\\frac{1}{2}\\,⇒\\,y=-\\frac{1}{5}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, the constraint condition $2{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}-2y=0$ implies\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2{x}^{2}=-\\frac{11}{25}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich gives a contradiction.\n\nTherefore, $f$ has possible extreme values at the points\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(0,2).\n$$\n\nWe evaluate $f$ at these points. These are given in the following table:\n\n| $(x,y)$ | $(0,0)$ | $(0,2)$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x,y)$ | $1$ | $-11$ |\n\nTherefore, the function reaches a maximum value at the point $(0,0)$ and its maximum value is $1.$",
